1
00:00:01,340 --> 00:00:06,292
Tokyo. Rain. Shibuya Crossing: thousands
of people, and almost no one shoves.

2
00:00:06,332 --> 00:00:09,922
Last year, Tokyo police
received four and a half billion

3
00:00:09,962 --> 00:00:12,580
yen in lost cash, handed in by strangers.

4
00:00:12,620 --> 00:00:14,849
Nearly twenty-nine million dollars.

5
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:20,893
That same year, Japan logged over
eighty thousand missing-person reports.

6
00:00:20,933 --> 00:00:24,072
How do people vanish in a
country that returns your wallet?

7
00:00:24,112 --> 00:00:26,551
And why is there a word
for death by overwork?

8
00:00:26,591 --> 00:00:29,029
The answer to the first
comes at the very end.

9
00:00:29,069 --> 00:00:30,538
And it's not what you think.

10
00:00:33,960 --> 00:00:35,215
Today, we'll find out:

11
00:00:36,100 --> 00:00:38,587
why napping in a meeting
here is almost a compliment;

12
00:00:41,420 --> 00:00:44,020
why many Japanese women
laugh behind their hands, and

13
00:00:44,060 --> 00:00:45,813
what black teeth have to do with it;

14
00:00:47,850 --> 00:00:50,136
what it costs to rent
a dad for an evening;

15
00:00:51,970 --> 00:00:54,505
why a train apologized for twenty seconds;

16
00:00:55,760 --> 00:00:58,874
where fluffy rabbits hop through
the ruins of a secret factory;

17
00:01:00,580 --> 00:01:04,830
whether Japanese women really go crazy for
Western men, and what the statistics say;

18
00:01:06,250 --> 00:01:10,513
why the most famous book on the samurai
code was written in English, in America;

19
00:01:12,800 --> 00:01:16,638
why some elderly Japanese women say
they feel calmer in prison than outside;

20
00:01:19,090 --> 00:01:23,164
what ramen, a hotel and getting here
cost, and where cash is still king;

21
00:01:25,590 --> 00:01:28,359
and who people call on a phone
that isn't connected to anything.

22
00:01:31,980 --> 00:01:36,353
And one more thing. There's an object
here that almost nobody comes back for.

23
00:01:36,393 --> 00:01:38,473
It's in every chapter. Spot it first?

24
00:01:38,513 --> 00:01:40,479
Drop the timestamp in the comments.

25
00:01:40,519 --> 00:01:43,173
The answer comes when
the last lanterns go out.

26
00:02:02,530 --> 00:02:04,214
Eight a.m. Tokyo goes to work.

27
00:02:04,254 --> 00:02:08,638
The train is packed, yet silent: phone
calls on trains just aren't done here.

28
00:02:08,678 --> 00:02:12,028
On the platform, people wait
at lines painted on the floor,

29
00:02:12,068 --> 00:02:14,441
and the doors open exactly at those lines.

30
00:02:14,481 --> 00:02:16,969
Many passengers doze,
and nobody wakes them.

31
00:02:17,009 --> 00:02:20,761
Why is sleeping in public no shame
here, even a sign of hard work?

32
00:02:20,801 --> 00:02:22,715
That's coming in the work chapter.

33
00:02:22,755 --> 00:02:25,530
And at rush hour, many
lines run women-only cars,

34
00:02:25,570 --> 00:02:28,690
because groping happens in
the crush. That's Japan too.

35
00:02:32,720 --> 00:02:36,195
These platforms made one of the
most Japanese headlines ever.

36
00:02:36,235 --> 00:02:39,595
In November twenty seventeen,
a Tokyo-area railway formally

37
00:02:39,635 --> 00:02:42,246
apologized: a train left
twenty seconds early.

38
00:02:42,286 --> 00:02:45,588
Six months later, a western
railway apologized for leaving

39
00:02:45,628 --> 00:02:48,930
twenty-five seconds early,
calling it 'truly inexcusable'.

40
00:02:51,030 --> 00:02:53,715
Twenty-five seconds. Truly inexcusable.

41
00:03:00,970 --> 00:03:03,697
If a train is late anyway,
the station hands out a

42
00:03:03,737 --> 00:03:05,689
delay certificate to show your boss.

43
00:03:05,729 --> 00:03:09,175
And on the Tokaido line, the
first Shinkansen line, the average

44
00:03:09,215 --> 00:03:12,108
delay over a whole year is
under a minute and a half.

45
00:03:12,148 --> 00:03:13,989
Typhoons and earthquakes included.

46
00:03:16,250 --> 00:03:19,839
Watch the driver. He points at
the signal and calls it out loud.

47
00:03:19,879 --> 00:03:22,730
It looks like a game, but
Japanese studies say this

48
00:03:22,770 --> 00:03:25,508
gesture cuts errors by
about eighty-five percent.

49
00:03:25,548 --> 00:03:30,214
In sixty years, not one Shinkansen passenger
has died in a derailment or collision.

50
00:03:33,200 --> 00:03:36,197
So here's the riddle: why
does everything work here?

51
00:03:36,237 --> 00:03:38,300
Take the simplest example. A wallet.

52
00:03:39,310 --> 00:03:42,322
In Japan, lost things go
to the police: a precinct,

53
00:03:42,362 --> 00:03:44,537
or a tiny corner police box, a koban.

54
00:03:44,577 --> 00:03:47,768
The law is simple: items are
kept three months, and if

55
00:03:47,808 --> 00:03:50,521
nobody claims them, they
can go to the finder.

56
00:03:50,561 --> 00:03:54,411
In twenty twenty-five, Tokyo's
lost and found took in almost five

57
00:03:54,451 --> 00:03:57,284
million items: wallets,
phones, keys, documents.

58
00:03:57,324 --> 00:04:01,294
Almost three quarters of the found
cash made it back to its owners.

59
00:04:01,334 --> 00:04:04,765
The year's biggest find:
twenty-seven million yen in cash,

60
00:04:04,805 --> 00:04:08,894
about a hundred and seventy thousand
dollars. That got handed in too.

61
00:04:13,370 --> 00:04:14,716
Why don't people keep it?

62
00:04:14,756 --> 00:04:17,933
There's no single answer, but
some things help explain it.

63
00:04:17,973 --> 00:04:20,872
By law, the finder can claim
a reward from the owner:

64
00:04:20,912 --> 00:04:22,868
five to twenty percent of the value.

65
00:04:22,908 --> 00:04:25,918
There are thousands of police
boxes across the country.

66
00:04:25,958 --> 00:04:29,468
And the habit starts at school:
kids serve lunch themselves, and

67
00:04:29,508 --> 00:04:32,296
after class they clean the
classrooms and hallways.

68
00:04:32,336 --> 00:04:36,510
Since nineteen ninety-one, TV has even
run a show where toddlers go shopping

69
00:04:36,550 --> 00:04:39,782
alone for the first time, with
a hidden film crew close by.

70
00:04:39,822 --> 00:04:41,723
Honesty here isn't just upbringing.

71
00:04:41,763 --> 00:04:44,607
When almost everyone does
it, life is simply easier.

72
00:04:49,640 --> 00:04:51,653
Out on the street, another oddity.

73
00:04:51,693 --> 00:04:55,697
An iron box full of coins stands
alone, at night, on an empty road.

74
00:04:55,737 --> 00:04:58,414
And people say it's
almost never broken into.

75
00:04:58,454 --> 00:05:02,760
Japan has nearly four million vending
machines. Some sell hot oden soup.

76
00:05:02,800 --> 00:05:05,718
And some are special: in
an earthquake or another

77
00:05:05,758 --> 00:05:08,133
disaster, they hand out drinks for free.

78
00:05:12,000 --> 00:05:16,244
There are almost no trash cans on the
streets, and almost no litter either.

79
00:05:16,284 --> 00:05:19,271
People often say the bins
vanished after the nineteen

80
00:05:19,311 --> 00:05:23,098
ninety-five gas attack on the Tokyo
subway. That's just one reason.

81
00:05:23,138 --> 00:05:26,467
Habit does the rest: you carry
your trash home and sort it.

82
00:05:26,507 --> 00:05:31,094
The town of Kamikatsu sorts it into
forty-five types and recycles eighty percent.

83
00:05:33,820 --> 00:05:35,221
First, some quick prices.

84
00:05:35,261 --> 00:05:38,047
A subway ride starts at
a hundred and eighty yen.

85
00:05:38,087 --> 00:05:42,544
Simple rule: a hundred and fifty to a
hundred and sixty yen is about a dollar.

86
00:05:42,584 --> 00:05:44,620
So the subway is just over a dollar.

87
00:05:44,660 --> 00:05:47,502
Convenience-store coffee:
a hundred and forty to a

88
00:05:47,542 --> 00:05:49,693
hundred and sixty yen, about a dollar.

89
00:05:49,733 --> 00:05:54,191
And dinner from the same store, onigiri
rice triangles, a fluffy egg sandwich,

90
00:05:54,231 --> 00:05:57,708
hot oden by the register, is a
tourist attraction of its own.

91
00:06:01,560 --> 00:06:03,491
Everything runs like clockwork here.

92
00:06:03,531 --> 00:06:05,079
But what does the clock cost?

93
00:06:05,119 --> 00:06:09,679
We checked everything, from a bowl of rice
to a fish pricier than a Tokyo apartment.

94
00:06:18,660 --> 00:06:20,649
January fifth, twenty twenty-six.

95
00:06:20,689 --> 00:06:23,724
Toyosu fish market, the
first auction of the year.

96
00:06:23,764 --> 00:06:26,860
A two hundred and forty-three
kilogram bluefin tuna

97
00:06:26,900 --> 00:06:29,504
sells for five hundred
and ten million yen.

98
00:06:29,544 --> 00:06:32,456
About three point two
million dollars, a record.

99
00:06:33,720 --> 00:06:34,935
Relax, it's a show.

100
00:06:34,975 --> 00:06:40,086
The year's first tuna is an ad and a
good-luck charm, bought by a sushi chain.

101
00:06:40,126 --> 00:06:42,002
Ordinary tuna is far cheaper.

102
00:06:42,490 --> 00:06:45,473
Surprise: much of Japan
costs less than you'd think.

103
00:06:45,513 --> 00:06:50,007
Gyudon, a beef-and-rice bowl, is four
hundred and eighty yen at a chain diner.

104
00:06:50,047 --> 00:06:52,333
About three dollars for a filling lunch.

105
00:06:53,110 --> 00:06:57,454
Ramen is trickier. In twenty twenty-five,
the average ramen-shop bill topped a

106
00:06:57,494 --> 00:07:00,264
thousand yen for the first
time, over six dollars.

107
00:07:00,304 --> 00:07:04,142
Conveyor-belt sushi starts at about
a hundred and twenty yen a plate.

108
00:07:04,182 --> 00:07:05,772
Hundred-yen sushi is history.

109
00:07:06,610 --> 00:07:07,159
Transport.

110
00:07:07,199 --> 00:07:11,751
A one-day Tokyo subway pass: seven hundred
yen, about four and a half dollars.

111
00:07:11,791 --> 00:07:16,108
A Tokyo taxi's base fare: about five
hundred yen, just over three dollars.

112
00:07:16,148 --> 00:07:19,464
The Shinkansen from Tokyo to
Kyoto: fourteen thousand one

113
00:07:19,504 --> 00:07:22,172
hundred and seventy yen,
about ninety dollars.

114
00:07:22,212 --> 00:07:26,588
A one-week rail pass for tourists starts
at fifty thousand yen, about three

115
00:07:26,628 --> 00:07:30,121
hundred and twenty dollars, and
only pays off on long trips.

116
00:07:33,150 --> 00:07:37,418
Fifteen more minutes on the Shinkansen
from Kyoto, and you're in Osaka.

117
00:07:37,458 --> 00:07:41,969
The Dotonbori district: neon over the
canal, a giant mechanical crab over a

118
00:07:42,009 --> 00:07:45,549
restaurant door, a line for
takoyaki fresh off the griddle.

119
00:07:45,589 --> 00:07:47,794
If Tokyo is order, Osaka is appetite.

120
00:07:47,834 --> 00:07:52,345
A beer in an ordinary izakaya: about
six hundred yen, roughly four dollars.

121
00:07:54,550 --> 00:07:58,976
A night in a big-chain business hotel in
spring twenty twenty-six averaged about

122
00:07:59,016 --> 00:08:03,274
fourteen thousand eight hundred yen,
roughly ninety-five dollars. And rising.

123
00:08:05,040 --> 00:08:06,811
Now a detail for the sharp-eyed.

124
00:08:06,851 --> 00:08:08,621
Himeji Castle, white as a heron.

125
00:08:08,661 --> 00:08:11,960
For residents of Himeji itself,
a ticket is a thousand yen.

126
00:08:11,000 --> 00:08:15,411
For everyone else, including
Japanese from other cities, it's

127
00:08:15,451 --> 00:08:18,184
two thousand five hundred,
about sixteen dollars.

128
00:08:18,224 --> 00:08:21,126
Climbing Mount Fuji has
cost four thousand yen since

129
00:08:21,166 --> 00:08:23,729
twenty twenty-five, about
twenty-five dollars.

130
00:08:23,769 --> 00:08:26,728
Popular places have learned
to charge visitors extra.

131
00:08:30,220 --> 00:08:32,765
Tourism still hit a
record: almost forty-three

132
00:08:32,805 --> 00:08:34,956
million visitors in twenty twenty-five.

133
00:08:34,996 --> 00:08:39,452
In Kyoto, they're already closing alleys:
since twenty twenty-four, tourists are

134
00:08:39,492 --> 00:08:43,947
banned from some lanes of the Gion district,
and residents' signs threaten fines

135
00:08:43,987 --> 00:08:47,206
of up to ten thousand yen for
filming in private passages.

136
00:08:47,246 --> 00:08:51,477
And in twenty twenty-six, arrivals fell
for the first time in years: after a

137
00:08:51,517 --> 00:08:55,017
spat with Tokyo, China advised
its citizens not to travel here.

138
00:08:57,490 --> 00:09:00,623
Japan draws people even from
the far side of the world,

139
00:09:00,663 --> 00:09:04,258
where the trip is long and
complicated. So it must be worth it.

140
00:09:04,298 --> 00:09:06,393
Here's how to get here, step by step.

141
00:09:11,410 --> 00:09:14,583
Not everyone needs a visa:
citizens of many countries,

142
00:09:14,623 --> 00:09:17,856
including most of Europe,
can skip it for a short trip.

143
00:09:17,896 --> 00:09:22,379
Others apply ahead: at an embassy, a
consulate, or in some countries online.

144
00:09:22,419 --> 00:09:26,663
Expect a bank statement, a day-by-day
itinerary and the usual documents.

145
00:09:26,703 --> 00:09:30,234
Rules depend on your passport,
so check the embassy website.

146
00:09:32,970 --> 00:09:35,099
Many countries fly direct to Japan.

147
00:09:35,139 --> 00:09:39,624
Handy connections go through Seoul,
Beijing, Shanghai, Istanbul or Dubai.

148
00:09:39,664 --> 00:09:42,289
Fares swing with the
season, so book early.

149
00:09:43,620 --> 00:09:46,934
Cards are accepted more and
more, but not everywhere: small

150
00:09:46,974 --> 00:09:50,686
shops, temples and rural buses
often need cash. So bring some yen.

151
00:09:50,726 --> 00:09:55,177
And the tourist transit card, by the way,
can be bought and reloaded with cash.

152
00:09:58,060 --> 00:09:59,177
Two changes this year.

153
00:09:59,217 --> 00:10:02,489
The departure tax tripled to
three thousand yen, about nineteen

154
00:10:02,529 --> 00:10:05,012
dollars, for tickets
bought starting July first.

155
00:10:05,052 --> 00:10:08,955
And since November first, you pay full
price in tax-free shops, and the ten

156
00:10:08,995 --> 00:10:12,688
percent tax comes back after customs
checks your purchase on departure.

157
00:10:16,010 --> 00:10:18,744
The honest downside: little
English is spoken here.

158
00:10:18,784 --> 00:10:22,498
In the English proficiency ranking,
Japan sits below eightieth place.

159
00:10:22,538 --> 00:10:24,891
Your phone's translator
is your best friend.

160
00:10:26,770 --> 00:10:28,978
Remember one rule from this chapter.

161
00:10:29,018 --> 00:10:31,976
Food and city transport:
cheaper than you think.

162
00:10:32,016 --> 00:10:35,036
Housing, long-distance
trains, taxis and anything

163
00:10:35,076 --> 00:10:37,597
touristy: expensive, and getting pricier.

164
00:10:38,370 --> 00:10:41,601
But the most valuable things in
Japan aren't for sale at all.

165
00:10:41,641 --> 00:10:44,765
There's an island no amount of
yen can buy you a ticket to.

166
00:10:44,805 --> 00:10:47,982
It holds eighty thousand
treasures, and one man lives there.

167
00:10:57,270 --> 00:11:01,571
The sacred island of Okinoshima lies
in the strait between Japan and Korea.

168
00:11:01,611 --> 00:11:03,249
Women may not set foot on it.

169
00:11:03,289 --> 00:11:05,332
Then again, men almost can't either.

170
00:11:05,372 --> 00:11:08,747
Once a year, about two hundred
men used to land here, first

171
00:11:08,787 --> 00:11:11,409
stripping and purifying
themselves in the sea.

172
00:11:11,449 --> 00:11:16,155
Since it joined the UNESCO list in twenty
seventeen, even that has been cancelled.

173
00:11:17,010 --> 00:11:19,328
One person lives on the island: a priest.

174
00:11:19,368 --> 00:11:21,687
Every ten days, another one replaces him.

175
00:11:21,727 --> 00:11:24,448
You can't take anything
away, not even a pebble.

176
00:11:24,488 --> 00:11:27,612
And about eighty thousand
objects have been found here,

177
00:11:27,652 --> 00:11:30,143
left between the fourth
and ninth centuries.

178
00:11:30,183 --> 00:11:32,904
Together they're designated
a National Treasure.

179
00:11:32,944 --> 00:11:35,665
A lost and found that
never gives anything back.

180
00:11:36,590 --> 00:11:38,651
Almost nobody gets onto this island.

181
00:11:38,691 --> 00:11:41,687
Our channel lets everyone
in: subscribe right below.

182
00:11:41,727 --> 00:11:44,139
No stripping or sea purification required.

183
00:11:46,090 --> 00:11:48,250
And here's the country's biggest find.

184
00:11:48,290 --> 00:11:52,996
In twenty twenty-three, Japan 'found' over
seven thousand islands in a single day.

185
00:11:53,036 --> 00:11:56,758
Nothing surfaced: they recounted
the islands on digital maps, and

186
00:11:56,798 --> 00:11:59,768
almost seven thousand became
over fourteen thousand.

187
00:12:00,620 --> 00:12:03,269
And there's an island
where everyone's welcome.

188
00:12:03,309 --> 00:12:04,757
Rabbit Island, Okunoshima.

189
00:12:04,797 --> 00:12:09,505
Hundreds of rabbits greet you, hopping
through the ruins of concrete factory halls.

190
00:12:09,545 --> 00:12:13,453
This island was once wiped off the
maps: from nineteen twenty-nine to

191
00:12:13,493 --> 00:12:17,687
nineteen forty-five, poison gas was
secretly made here. Thousands of tons.

192
00:12:18,490 --> 00:12:22,425
The rabbits are counted. In twenty
eighteen, there were over nine hundred.

193
00:12:22,465 --> 00:12:25,755
Then a die-off, and an animal-cruelty
probe by police: by late

194
00:12:25,795 --> 00:12:28,226
twenty twenty-four, two
hundred and sixty-two.

195
00:12:28,266 --> 00:12:31,073
In twenty twenty-five, almost
four hundred and fifty.

196
00:12:33,500 --> 00:12:36,142
Another island everyone
knows by its silhouette.

197
00:12:36,182 --> 00:12:39,272
Gunkanjima, 'Battleship
Island', a Mitsubishi coal mine.

198
00:12:39,312 --> 00:12:43,239
In nineteen fifty-nine, over five
thousand people lived here on a scrap

199
00:12:43,279 --> 00:12:46,257
of land just over six hectares,
roughly fifteen acres.

200
00:12:46,297 --> 00:12:49,107
One of the highest population
densities in history.

201
00:12:49,147 --> 00:12:53,130
In seventy-four the mine closed, and
within months the island was empty.

202
00:12:57,760 --> 00:12:59,124
Today it's a UNESCO site.

203
00:12:59,164 --> 00:13:00,583
And it has a dark chapter.

204
00:13:00,623 --> 00:13:04,738
Japan itself told UNESCO that Koreans
and people from other countries were

205
00:13:04,778 --> 00:13:08,612
brought to some of these sites and
forced to work against their will.

206
00:13:08,652 --> 00:13:11,868
And in twenty twenty-one,
UNESCO expressed 'strong regret'

207
00:13:11,908 --> 00:13:14,395
that the museum says
almost nothing about it.

208
00:13:18,510 --> 00:13:22,468
A question that trips up almost everyone:
who owns the summit of Mount Fuji?

209
00:13:22,508 --> 00:13:25,782
Not the state. Everything above
the eighth station belongs to a

210
00:13:25,822 --> 00:13:27,991
Shinto shrine at the foot of the mountain.

211
00:13:28,031 --> 00:13:31,357
The Supreme Court confirmed it
in nineteen seventy-four, but the

212
00:13:31,397 --> 00:13:33,830
land was only handed over
in two thousand four.

213
00:13:33,870 --> 00:13:36,723
Since twenty twenty-four,
the most popular trail admits

214
00:13:36,763 --> 00:13:38,827
no more than four thousand people a day.

215
00:13:38,867 --> 00:13:42,088
And even Fuji has started running
late: in twenty twenty-four,

216
00:13:42,128 --> 00:13:45,454
snow first fell on the summit
only on November sixth, the latest

217
00:13:45,494 --> 00:13:47,769
since records began in
eighteen ninety-four.

218
00:13:54,380 --> 00:13:58,985
And the nation's greatest treasures may
not have been seen by anyone for centuries.

219
00:13:59,025 --> 00:14:03,126
A mirror, a sword and a jewel: the
imperial regalia. They are never shown.

220
00:14:03,166 --> 00:14:06,764
Even at the twenty nineteen
ceremony, when a new emperor took the

221
00:14:06,804 --> 00:14:10,178
throne, the sword and the jewel
were carried in closed boxes.

222
00:14:10,218 --> 00:14:13,368
Historians say even the emperor
himself doesn't see them.

223
00:14:16,230 --> 00:14:19,100
The country's most polite
residents live in Nara.

224
00:14:19,140 --> 00:14:20,763
Nara's deer bow to tourists.

225
00:14:20,803 --> 00:14:22,248
Only it isn't politeness.

226
00:14:22,288 --> 00:14:26,761
Scientists found that Nara's deer learned
to bow for crackers, while deer on

227
00:14:26,801 --> 00:14:30,325
Miyajima island, where feeding
them is banned, bow far less.

228
00:14:30,365 --> 00:14:33,176
They aren't bowing to
you. They're training you.

229
00:14:35,350 --> 00:14:38,381
Deer numbers hit a record
high, almost seventeen hundred.

230
00:14:38,421 --> 00:14:41,074
In one year, they injured
a hundred and fifty-nine

231
00:14:41,114 --> 00:14:43,068
visitors: they're still wild animals.

232
00:14:43,108 --> 00:14:44,738
Don't tease them with crackers.

233
00:14:45,680 --> 00:14:47,774
But one mystery here, everyone can feel.

234
00:14:47,814 --> 00:14:50,336
In twenty twenty-five,
people in Japan felt four

235
00:14:50,376 --> 00:14:54,018
thousand four hundred and fifty-six
earthquakes. That's twelve a day.

236
00:14:54,058 --> 00:14:56,953
While you're watching this
film, somewhere in Japan the

237
00:14:56,993 --> 00:14:59,141
ground has quite possibly shaken already.

238
00:14:59,181 --> 00:15:03,356
The country gets about twenty percent of
the world's magnitude-six-plus quakes,

239
00:15:03,396 --> 00:15:06,825
though it has less than a third
of a percent of the world's land.

240
00:15:09,110 --> 00:15:12,068
In your shoes, we'd install
an alert app on our phone and

241
00:15:12,108 --> 00:15:14,488
check the hotel's
evacuation plan first thing.

242
00:15:14,528 --> 00:15:19,011
They take this seriously here: in August
twenty twenty-four, the weather agency issued

243
00:15:19,051 --> 00:15:22,798
its first-ever advisory about a
possible megaquake in the Nankai Trough.

244
00:15:22,838 --> 00:15:24,376
It stayed in force for a week.

245
00:15:28,890 --> 00:15:32,880
Yet every morning, people still get
up and go to work. What are they paid?

246
00:15:32,920 --> 00:15:34,895
And why do they doze off in meetings?

247
00:15:45,120 --> 00:15:49,738
Tokyo's minimum wage: twelve hundred and
eighty yen an hour, about eight dollars.

248
00:15:49,778 --> 00:15:53,418
The national average salary: about
four hundred and six thousand

249
00:15:53,458 --> 00:15:57,386
yen a month, roughly two thousand
six hundred dollars. Sounds decent.

250
00:15:57,426 --> 00:15:59,859
Now look at how people
work for that money.

251
00:16:01,490 --> 00:16:04,607
This man is asleep.
Mid-meeting, in plain sight.

252
00:16:04,647 --> 00:16:07,106
Here, that can almost be a compliment.

253
00:16:07,570 --> 00:16:11,026
There's even a name for it:
inemuri, 'sleeping while present'.

254
00:16:11,066 --> 00:16:14,805
Cambridge researcher Brigitte Steger
describes the unwritten rules.

255
00:16:14,845 --> 00:16:19,260
Nodding off in public is forgivable: it
can mean you've given your all at work.

256
00:16:19,300 --> 00:16:21,854
But only if you're not
in anyone's way and can

257
00:16:21,894 --> 00:16:26,027
instantly 'come back' to the conversation.
A new hire can't. The boss can.

258
00:16:27,790 --> 00:16:30,333
Now a twist that breaks
the main stereotype.

259
00:16:30,373 --> 00:16:32,799
The Japanese now work less than Americans.

260
00:16:32,839 --> 00:16:37,613
According to the OECD, in twenty twenty-four,
it was sixteen hundred and seventeen

261
00:16:37,653 --> 00:16:42,075
hours a year per worker, versus seventeen
hundred and ninety-six in America.

262
00:16:42,115 --> 00:16:45,480
In nineteen ninety, the Japanese
worked over two thousand.

263
00:16:45,520 --> 00:16:49,824
But the OECD itself warns: unpaid
overtime isn't counted in these figures.

264
00:16:53,650 --> 00:16:55,208
Sleep, though, is a problem.

265
00:16:55,248 --> 00:17:00,173
In a dataset the same organization published
in twenty nineteen, the Japanese slept the

266
00:17:00,213 --> 00:17:04,339
least of all member countries: seven
hours twenty-two minutes on average.

267
00:17:05,680 --> 00:17:09,844
In fiscal twenty twenty-five, the
government recognized thirteen hundred

268
00:17:09,884 --> 00:17:14,047
and ten cases where illness or death
was caused by work. Another record.

269
00:17:14,087 --> 00:17:17,667
The Japanese have a word for it:
karoshi, death from overwork.

270
00:17:20,080 --> 00:17:24,248
How, with fewer hours? Official hours
are only the tip of the iceberg.

271
00:17:24,288 --> 00:17:27,434
There's unpaid overtime:
every year, labor inspectors

272
00:17:27,474 --> 00:17:29,658
make companies pay back wages for it.

273
00:17:29,698 --> 00:17:32,483
There are after-work
drinks with the team that,

274
00:17:32,523 --> 00:17:35,609
for years, you just didn't
turn down. Paid vacation?

275
00:17:35,649 --> 00:17:37,232
People use only two thirds.

276
00:17:37,272 --> 00:17:40,418
And most recognized cases
today aren't heart attacks,

277
00:17:40,458 --> 00:17:42,942
but mental disorders: stress and pressure.

278
00:17:44,960 --> 00:17:46,378
Karoshi has a human face.

279
00:17:46,418 --> 00:17:49,409
Matsuri Takahashi, twenty-four,
in her first year at

280
00:17:49,449 --> 00:17:51,800
the country's biggest advertising agency.

281
00:17:51,840 --> 00:17:54,948
In twenty fifteen, after a
month in which her overtime

282
00:17:54,988 --> 00:17:57,630
topped a hundred hours,
she took her own life.

283
00:17:57,670 --> 00:18:00,195
The government ruled
her death work-related.

284
00:18:00,235 --> 00:18:04,335
Her story shook the country and pushed
a reform: since twenty nineteen,

285
00:18:04,375 --> 00:18:08,416
overtime has been capped by law for
the first time, no more than seven

286
00:18:08,456 --> 00:18:11,856
hundred and twenty hours a
year, even in exceptional cases.

287
00:18:16,930 --> 00:18:20,995
Where does a Japanese worker go home
to? A shoebox, they say. Let's check.

288
00:18:21,035 --> 00:18:23,714
A big twenty twenty-three
survey puts the average

289
00:18:23,754 --> 00:18:27,819
Japanese home at almost ninety-one
square meters. Bigger than you thought?

290
00:18:27,859 --> 00:18:31,980
But that's a national average, and the
countryside is full of roomy houses.

291
00:18:34,280 --> 00:18:34,799
Now Tokyo.

292
00:18:34,839 --> 00:18:39,049
An apartment of up to thirty square
meters rents for about a hundred and ten

293
00:18:39,089 --> 00:18:42,515
thousand yen a month on average,
around seven hundred dollars.

294
00:18:42,555 --> 00:18:45,591
A typical studio: fifteen to
twenty-five square meters.

295
00:18:45,631 --> 00:18:48,498
And the bravest rent a
three-tatami room: under five

296
00:18:48,538 --> 00:18:50,511
square meters, with a sleeping loft.

297
00:18:52,200 --> 00:18:55,877
For those who can't afford even
that, there's an internet-café booth.

298
00:18:55,917 --> 00:18:59,110
A Tokyo survey from almost ten
years ago found about fifteen

299
00:18:59,150 --> 00:19:02,073
thousand people sleeping in
such booths on a weeknight.

300
00:19:02,113 --> 00:19:04,928
About four thousand of them
had nowhere else to live.

301
00:19:09,060 --> 00:19:11,746
Quitting a job here is so
hard that there are firms

302
00:19:11,786 --> 00:19:13,991
that will tell your boss 'I quit' for you.

303
00:19:14,031 --> 00:19:16,556
The service costs about
twenty-two thousand yen.

304
00:19:16,596 --> 00:19:19,442
One in six people who changed
jobs in the year through

305
00:19:19,482 --> 00:19:21,794
June twenty twenty-four
used such an agency.

306
00:19:21,834 --> 00:19:25,001
In twenty twenty-five, police
raided the best-known of them.

307
00:19:25,041 --> 00:19:28,422
And in twenty twenty-six, its
head was arrested, on suspicion of

308
00:19:28,462 --> 00:19:31,682
steering clients to lawyers for
money, which the law forbids.

309
00:19:36,290 --> 00:19:39,972
But Japan's strangest business
isn't leaving. It's showing up.

310
00:19:40,012 --> 00:19:41,653
Here, you can rent a person.

311
00:19:41,693 --> 00:19:45,856
Yuichi Ishii's company rents out
actors to play relatives and friends.

312
00:19:45,896 --> 00:19:49,518
A 'dad' for an evening or a
wedding: from twenty thousand yen

313
00:19:49,558 --> 00:19:52,400
plus travel, about a
hundred and thirty dollars.

314
00:19:52,440 --> 00:19:54,501
A wedding guest: from ten thousand.

315
00:19:54,541 --> 00:19:57,863
And someone to apologize for
you: from fifteen thousand.

316
00:20:00,460 --> 00:20:03,706
In twenty twenty-five, Hollywood
even made a movie about

317
00:20:03,746 --> 00:20:06,346
this kind of rental,
starring Brendan Fraser.

318
00:20:06,386 --> 00:20:09,045
And a rented dad has one
huge advantage: he'll

319
00:20:09,085 --> 00:20:11,393
never ask when the grandkids are coming.

320
00:20:11,433 --> 00:20:12,918
That's not in the package.

321
00:20:12,958 --> 00:20:18,199
Takanobu Nishimoto went further: he began
renting himself out as an 'ossan', a middle-aged

322
00:20:18,239 --> 00:20:22,659
guy, for a thousand yen an hour, about
six dollars, just to listen and talk.

323
00:20:22,699 --> 00:20:25,358
Ten years on, there were
about seventy ossans.

324
00:20:27,060 --> 00:20:30,821
But once a year, even this office
goes outside. Spring, cherry blossoms.

325
00:20:30,861 --> 00:20:35,204
The bloom forecast here is news the whole
country waits for: meteorologists started

326
00:20:35,244 --> 00:20:39,164
predicting the date back in the fifties,
and now private companies compete.

327
00:20:39,204 --> 00:20:42,807
At some firms, the newest hire is
sent out in the morning to hold the

328
00:20:42,847 --> 00:20:45,605
best spot under a tree for
the team's evening picnic.

329
00:20:48,390 --> 00:20:50,483
Even spring here is tracked to the day.

330
00:20:50,523 --> 00:20:53,273
In Kyoto, cherry bloom
dates were recorded for over

331
00:20:53,313 --> 00:20:55,406
twelve hundred years, in court diaries.

332
00:20:55,446 --> 00:20:58,250
And in twenty twenty-one,
the trees bloomed on March

333
00:20:58,290 --> 00:21:00,930
twenty-sixth, earlier than
ever in all that time.

334
00:21:00,970 --> 00:21:04,923
In autumn, the country follows the
'front' of red maples just as closely.

335
00:21:07,200 --> 00:21:09,282
Cherry blossoms last one week a year.

336
00:21:09,322 --> 00:21:11,806
So how do you stay sane
the other fifty-one?

337
00:21:11,846 --> 00:21:15,649
The Japanese long ago invented a
place where hurrying is forbidden.

338
00:21:15,689 --> 00:21:18,231
Even for a samurai.
Especially for a samurai.

339
00:21:29,930 --> 00:21:32,030
This chapter has one rule: no rushing.

340
00:21:32,070 --> 00:21:34,733
And the Japanese phrase
for a meeting like this?

341
00:21:34,773 --> 00:21:36,704
We'll save it for the final minute.

342
00:21:38,760 --> 00:21:40,358
You arrive a little early.

343
00:21:40,398 --> 00:21:43,570
You put on fresh white tabi
socks and step onto the

344
00:21:43,610 --> 00:21:48,294
roji, the garden path to the tea house.
Moss. Flat stones. A stone lantern.

345
00:21:48,334 --> 00:21:50,624
The city beyond the fence fades away.

346
00:21:53,710 --> 00:21:55,786
By the path sits a low stone basin.

347
00:21:55,826 --> 00:21:59,959
To reach the water with a bamboo
ladle, you must crouch. Even a lord.

348
00:21:59,999 --> 00:22:04,494
Wash your hands, rinse your mouth. A
bamboo spout trickles. The only sound.

349
00:22:07,200 --> 00:22:11,233
Then the door, if you can call it
that: an opening below the waist.

350
00:22:11,273 --> 00:22:15,246
Shoes stay outside. You crawl in
on your knees. A sword won't fit.

351
00:22:15,286 --> 00:22:17,921
It stays outside too.
Inside, all are equal.

352
00:22:21,070 --> 00:22:25,540
The room is tiny. The only surviving tea
room by the great master Sen no Rikyu

353
00:22:25,580 --> 00:22:28,084
is just two tatami, and
a National Treasure.

354
00:22:28,124 --> 00:22:31,206
First you look at the alcove:
a scroll and one flower,

355
00:22:31,246 --> 00:22:33,461
chosen for the occasion and the season.

356
00:22:38,800 --> 00:22:42,676
The old Japanese calendar has
seventy-two seasons, five days each.

357
00:22:42,716 --> 00:22:46,711
In early November, for instance,
it says 'camellias begin to bloom'.

358
00:22:46,751 --> 00:22:50,272
For centuries, the Japanese
marked time to within five days.

359
00:22:51,810 --> 00:22:53,339
Silence. Only the kettle.

360
00:22:53,379 --> 00:22:55,849
Its sound is called 'wind in the pines'.

361
00:22:55,889 --> 00:22:58,672
First you're served a
sweet, then bitter tea.

362
00:22:58,712 --> 00:23:02,500
Sweet first, then bitter: that's
how the bitterness opens up.

363
00:23:06,150 --> 00:23:09,162
Thick tea is drunk from
one bowl, passed around.

364
00:23:09,202 --> 00:23:12,978
You turn the bowl so as not to
drink from its 'face': out of

365
00:23:13,018 --> 00:23:17,684
modesty, its most beautiful side turns
away from you. A sip. Wipe the rim.

366
00:23:17,724 --> 00:23:22,898
Pass it on. Then admire the bowl itself:
the glaze, the crackle, the maker's hand.

367
00:23:22,938 --> 00:23:27,986
If a bowl once broke, it may be mended
with gold lacquer. The seam isn't hidden.

368
00:23:28,026 --> 00:23:28,812
It's admired.

369
00:23:33,030 --> 00:23:34,574
This beauty had its price.

370
00:23:34,614 --> 00:23:39,022
Sen no Rikyu, credited with this door
of equality, was ordered in fifteen

371
00:23:39,062 --> 00:23:43,775
ninety-one by his ruler, Toyotomi
Hideyoshi, to commit seppuku. And he obeyed.

372
00:23:43,815 --> 00:23:45,055
Why is still debated.

373
00:23:45,095 --> 00:23:49,381
In the best-known version, Rikyu
placed a wooden statue of himself on a

374
00:23:49,421 --> 00:23:53,403
temple gate, so the ruler would
have had to pass beneath its feet.

375
00:23:58,470 --> 00:24:01,215
A full tea gathering
lasts up to four hours.

376
00:24:01,255 --> 00:24:04,380
Tourists get a short one,
half an hour or an hour.

377
00:24:04,420 --> 00:24:06,026
But both end the same way.

378
00:24:06,066 --> 00:24:08,622
The host bows at the door, and that's it.

379
00:24:08,662 --> 00:24:10,964
This meeting will never happen again.

380
00:24:11,004 --> 00:24:15,585
The Japanese have a phrase for it:
ichigo ichie. 'One time, one meeting'.

381
00:24:20,310 --> 00:24:25,085
A meeting happens once. So how do people
here find someone to share a lifetime with?

382
00:24:25,125 --> 00:24:26,805
Why are fewer people marrying?

383
00:24:26,845 --> 00:24:30,589
And why do Japanese women so often
hide their laugh behind a hand?

384
00:24:41,100 --> 00:24:43,339
Start with a gesture everyone knows.

385
00:24:43,379 --> 00:24:45,556
A girl laughs and covers her mouth.

386
00:24:45,596 --> 00:24:48,278
The usual answer:
modesty, etiquette. True.

387
00:24:48,318 --> 00:24:50,051
But there's an older theory.

388
00:24:50,091 --> 00:24:55,243
For centuries, noblewomen, and later mostly
married women, dyed their teeth black.

389
00:24:55,283 --> 00:24:58,726
It was called ohaguro, and
it was considered beautiful.

390
00:24:59,280 --> 00:25:03,677
In eighteen seventy, the government banned
teeth-blackening among the nobility.

391
00:25:03,717 --> 00:25:06,766
In eighteen seventy-three,
the empress herself appeared

392
00:25:06,806 --> 00:25:09,012
with white teeth, and the fashion faded.

393
00:25:09,052 --> 00:25:12,045
Some say hiding a smile is
partly an echo of that era.

394
00:25:13,540 --> 00:25:17,973
In the twenty-tens, fashion flipped:
a 'crooked' fang, yaeba, became cute.

395
00:25:18,013 --> 00:25:19,787
Clinics even fitted fake ones.

396
00:25:19,827 --> 00:25:22,265
Elsewhere, people pay for straight teeth.

397
00:25:22,305 --> 00:25:25,106
Here, for a while, they
paid for a crooked one.

398
00:25:25,750 --> 00:25:27,698
Beauty in Japan is serious business.

399
00:25:27,738 --> 00:25:31,068
In twenty twenty-five, over four
point three million cosmetic

400
00:25:31,108 --> 00:25:34,051
procedures were done here,
from injections to surgery.

401
00:25:34,091 --> 00:25:36,702
That same year, doctors
proposed a new diagnosis

402
00:25:36,742 --> 00:25:38,690
for young women who are underweight.

403
00:25:38,730 --> 00:25:41,065
Thinness here has become
a medical problem.

404
00:25:44,060 --> 00:25:48,988
The old beauty ideal: pale skin, hence
summer parasols. Now men carry them too.

405
00:25:49,028 --> 00:25:54,144
Not for beauty, but to survive the heat:
in August twenty twenty-five, Isesaki hit

406
00:25:54,184 --> 00:25:59,427
almost forty-two degrees Celsius, a hundred
and seven Fahrenheit. A national record.

407
00:26:00,180 --> 00:26:00,730
Now, love.

408
00:26:00,770 --> 00:26:05,269
In nineteen eighty, one in forty Japanese
men had never married by age fifty.

409
00:26:05,309 --> 00:26:08,747
Now, per the twenty twenty
census, it's twenty-eight men in

410
00:26:08,787 --> 00:26:11,283
a hundred, and eighteen
women in a hundred.

411
00:26:14,660 --> 00:26:17,291
Tokyo's government launched
its own dating app.

412
00:26:17,331 --> 00:26:20,758
To sign up, you need proof you're
single and proof of income.

413
00:26:20,798 --> 00:26:25,248
Romance, Japanese style. About thirty-six
thousand people have already applied.

414
00:26:28,010 --> 00:26:31,368
For many married couples, life
runs side by side, but apart.

415
00:26:31,408 --> 00:26:34,482
In a contraceptive maker's
survey, almost two thirds of

416
00:26:34,522 --> 00:26:37,087
married people said their
marriage is sexless.

417
00:26:37,127 --> 00:26:38,617
A survey, not medical data.

418
00:26:38,657 --> 00:26:42,920
But think of the working hours and tiny
apartments, and it makes more sense.

419
00:26:43,920 --> 00:26:46,897
Cramped homes also explain
Japan's famous love hotels.

420
00:26:46,937 --> 00:26:50,193
Book a couple of hours, called
a 'rest', or stay the night.

421
00:26:50,233 --> 00:26:54,606
At the entrance there's often a panel of
room photos, so you barely see anyone.

422
00:26:54,646 --> 00:26:57,455
When several generations
share a small, thin-walled

423
00:26:57,495 --> 00:26:59,913
apartment, a couple has
nowhere to be alone.

424
00:26:59,953 --> 00:27:01,310
Married couples come too.

425
00:27:06,550 --> 00:27:08,734
Infidelity here has its own word: furin.

426
00:27:08,774 --> 00:27:13,127
There are no reliable numbers: different
surveys give wildly different answers.

427
00:27:13,167 --> 00:27:14,795
But one detail explains a lot.

428
00:27:14,835 --> 00:27:18,465
Under Japanese law, a betrayed
spouse can sue the lover for money.

429
00:27:18,505 --> 00:27:22,524
No wonder cheating cases are among
the most common jobs for private eyes.

430
00:27:22,564 --> 00:27:25,582
And a celebrity caught cheating
often apologizes to the

431
00:27:25,622 --> 00:27:28,140
nation at a press
conference, with a deep bow.

432
00:27:32,070 --> 00:27:36,582
Some customs are funny. On Valentine's
Day in Japan, women give chocolate to men,

433
00:27:36,622 --> 00:27:39,055
and often to coworkers,
'out of obligation'.

434
00:27:39,095 --> 00:27:42,876
In twenty eighteen, the chocolate
maker Godiva bought a full page in

435
00:27:42,916 --> 00:27:45,854
Japan's leading business
paper and asked people to...

436
00:27:45,894 --> 00:27:47,709
stop giving obligation chocolate.

437
00:27:50,280 --> 00:27:52,558
After the wedding, one surname for both.

438
00:27:52,598 --> 00:27:55,514
The law requires it, and
in about ninety-four cases

439
00:27:55,554 --> 00:27:57,659
out of a hundred, the wife takes his.

440
00:27:57,699 --> 00:28:02,063
A Japanese professor half-jokingly
calculated: if the law stays, by the year

441
00:28:02,103 --> 00:28:05,599
twenty-five thirty-one, everyone
in Japan will be named Sato.

442
00:28:06,370 --> 00:28:09,388
Japan even has its own love
doodle: an umbrella, with

443
00:28:09,428 --> 00:28:11,638
two names on either side of the handle.

444
00:28:11,678 --> 00:28:13,138
Usually to tease a couple.

445
00:28:13,178 --> 00:28:15,100
It's called an 'umbrella for two'.

446
00:28:17,400 --> 00:28:21,960
A stereotype tourists love: are
Japanese women crazy about Western men?

447
00:28:21,000 --> 00:28:24,551
Let's look not at bars, but at weddings.

448
00:28:25,120 --> 00:28:29,069
In a year, almost seven thousand
Japanese women married foreigners.

449
00:28:29,109 --> 00:28:31,391
Most often Koreans: almost one in four.

450
00:28:31,431 --> 00:28:34,784
Americans come second, but
together with the British they

451
00:28:34,824 --> 00:28:37,225
make up about one in five such marriages.

452
00:28:37,265 --> 00:28:39,725
So 'everyone wants a Westerner' is a myth.

453
00:28:39,765 --> 00:28:44,667
It rests on tourists' impressions in
Roppongi's late-night bars, not on statistics.

454
00:28:45,180 --> 00:28:49,579
Longtime foreign residents often tell
major media the same thing: curiosity

455
00:28:49,619 --> 00:28:53,249
about you is huge, but it's not
an invitation into the family.

456
00:28:54,270 --> 00:28:58,434
The word 'foreigner' has another side,
and the government itself measured it.

457
00:28:58,474 --> 00:29:01,655
In a Justice Ministry survey
from twenty sixteen, over four

458
00:29:01,695 --> 00:29:04,221
thousand foreigners
described their experience.

459
00:29:04,261 --> 00:29:06,950
Of those looking for
housing, almost forty percent

460
00:29:06,990 --> 00:29:09,134
were turned down just for being foreign.

461
00:29:09,174 --> 00:29:11,045
One in four had been refused a job.

462
00:29:11,085 --> 00:29:14,430
And almost three quarters of
those affected never reported it.

463
00:29:17,040 --> 00:29:19,576
In twenty twenty-four,
three residents of Japan

464
00:29:19,616 --> 00:29:21,713
with foreign roots sued the government.

465
00:29:21,753 --> 00:29:24,508
They say police stop them
because of how they look.

466
00:29:24,548 --> 00:29:27,029
One says he's been stopped
over seventy times.

467
00:29:27,069 --> 00:29:30,592
And Japan still has no general
law against racial discrimination.

468
00:29:33,420 --> 00:29:35,492
Black Japanese have their own story.

469
00:29:35,532 --> 00:29:39,072
Ariana Miyamoto, daughter of a
Japanese mother and an African

470
00:29:39,112 --> 00:29:42,944
American father, became Miss
Universe Japan in twenty fifteen, and

471
00:29:42,984 --> 00:29:45,702
was promptly told she
wasn't 'Japanese enough'.

472
00:29:45,742 --> 00:29:48,049
Tennis star Naomi Osaka plays for Japan.

473
00:29:48,089 --> 00:29:52,743
In twenty nineteen, a noodle maker
apologized for an ad that lightened her skin.

474
00:29:54,970 --> 00:29:56,986
But stopping there wouldn't be fair.

475
00:29:57,026 --> 00:29:59,556
People say Japan doesn't
accept Black people.

476
00:29:59,596 --> 00:30:02,697
Yet in twenty twenty-one,
the country chose Naomi Osaka

477
00:30:02,737 --> 00:30:04,925
to light the Olympic cauldron in Tokyo.

478
00:30:04,965 --> 00:30:08,294
And basketball star Rui Hachimura,
son of a Japanese mother

479
00:30:08,334 --> 00:30:11,036
and a father from Benin,
is a national favorite.

480
00:30:11,076 --> 00:30:13,206
Pain and pride live side by side here.

481
00:30:16,920 --> 00:30:20,416
Japan now has a record number
of foreign residents: over four

482
00:30:20,456 --> 00:30:23,373
million, more than three
percent of the population.

483
00:30:23,413 --> 00:30:26,039
In twenty twenty-five,
a party with the slogan

484
00:30:26,079 --> 00:30:28,822
'Japanese First' won
fourteen upper-house seats.

485
00:30:28,862 --> 00:30:30,967
Immigration is now big politics here.

486
00:30:32,740 --> 00:30:35,260
Work, loneliness, other people's stares...

487
00:30:35,300 --> 00:30:40,074
To hide from all of it, many Japanese
escape into a world they drew themselves.

488
00:30:48,030 --> 00:30:52,793
Akihabara, the capital of Japanese
obsessions: anime, manga, games, figurines.

489
00:30:52,833 --> 00:30:57,472
And it's huge money. In twenty twenty-five,
Japan's anime market topped four

490
00:30:57,512 --> 00:31:00,674
trillion yen for the first
time, by early estimates.

491
00:31:00,714 --> 00:31:03,629
More than half that money
now comes from abroad.

492
00:31:04,110 --> 00:31:08,768
Now the paradox. Animation studio staff
aged twenty to twenty-four, in a twenty

493
00:31:08,808 --> 00:31:12,812
twenty-three survey, earned about
two million yen a year on average.

494
00:31:12,852 --> 00:31:16,915
That's about thirteen thousand
dollars, just over a thousand a month.

495
00:31:16,955 --> 00:31:21,434
Today, Tokyo's full-time minimum wage
pays more than two and a half million.

496
00:31:21,474 --> 00:31:24,467
A trillion-yen industry
paid its youngest less than

497
00:31:24,507 --> 00:31:26,608
a cashier on minimum wage earns now.

498
00:31:26,648 --> 00:31:29,700
The good news: working hours
at studios have dropped

499
00:31:29,740 --> 00:31:31,900
noticeably over almost fifteen years.

500
00:31:33,070 --> 00:31:35,815
This is where you see how
many share this dream.

501
00:31:35,855 --> 00:31:39,355
Comiket, a giant fair of comics
drawn by the fans themselves.

502
00:31:39,395 --> 00:31:42,256
In August twenty twenty-six,
about two hundred and

503
00:31:42,296 --> 00:31:44,229
sixty thousand came over two days.

504
00:31:45,510 --> 00:31:46,960
Anime even changes the map.

505
00:31:47,000 --> 00:31:51,210
After a twenty oh-seven series about
schoolgirls, drawn from real places, the

506
00:31:51,250 --> 00:31:55,570
small Washinomiya shrine near Tokyo went
from ninety thousand New Year visitors

507
00:31:55,610 --> 00:31:58,605
to four hundred and seventy
thousand a few years later.

508
00:31:58,645 --> 00:32:02,248
And a railroad crossing in Kamakura
from a famous basketball anime

509
00:32:02,288 --> 00:32:05,007
drew such crowds that the
city had to hire guards.

510
00:32:06,030 --> 00:32:07,769
Sometimes one cat is enough.

511
00:32:07,809 --> 00:32:12,598
In twenty oh-seven, Tama the cat became
stationmaster of tiny Kishi station.

512
00:32:12,638 --> 00:32:14,250
The line was due to close.

513
00:32:14,290 --> 00:32:16,220
Then tourists came for the cat.

514
00:32:16,260 --> 00:32:20,286
Her first-year economic impact:
about one point one billion yen.

515
00:32:21,380 --> 00:32:23,452
The world of hobbies has a gray area.

516
00:32:23,492 --> 00:32:27,391
Pachinko: halls where metal balls
clatter down through game machines.

517
00:32:27,431 --> 00:32:31,216
A hall can't pay winnings in cash,
so balls are swapped for prizes,

518
00:32:31,256 --> 00:32:34,070
and prizes, at a separate
window nearby, for cash.

519
00:32:34,110 --> 00:32:36,011
It's called the three-shop system.

520
00:32:36,051 --> 00:32:40,064
Pachinko halls have shrunk by almost
two thirds: over eighteen thousand

521
00:32:40,104 --> 00:32:43,432
in nineteen ninety-five, about
six and a half thousand now.

522
00:32:47,440 --> 00:32:52,221
On every Akihabara corner: gacha, capsule
machines where a coin buys a random toy.

523
00:32:52,261 --> 00:32:55,161
You never know what you'll
get. So you spin again.

524
00:32:56,610 --> 00:32:59,092
In anime, the hero is so often a warrior.

525
00:32:59,132 --> 00:33:02,107
For Japan, the samurai is
almost a national hero.

526
00:33:02,147 --> 00:33:05,490
But where did the famous
samurai code really come from?

527
00:33:05,530 --> 00:33:07,643
The answer starts across the ocean.

528
00:33:17,170 --> 00:33:19,401
Bushido, 'the way of the warrior'.

529
00:33:19,441 --> 00:33:21,738
Honor, loyalty, death without fear.

530
00:33:21,778 --> 00:33:27,214
The most famous book about this code came
out in eighteen ninety-nine. In English.

531
00:33:27,254 --> 00:33:30,953
Its author, Inazo Nitobe, a
Japanese man, wrote it while

532
00:33:30,993 --> 00:33:33,558
living in America, for Western readers.

533
00:33:35,060 --> 00:33:38,599
Historians, such as Oleg Benesch
in his Oxford book, argue that the

534
00:33:38,639 --> 00:33:42,177
'chivalrous' samurai code largely
took shape in the Meiji era, just

535
00:33:42,217 --> 00:33:44,528
as the samurai were
disappearing as a class.

536
00:33:44,568 --> 00:33:47,198
Samurai were only a few
percent of the population.

537
00:33:47,238 --> 00:33:50,777
And in eighteen seventy-six, they
were banned from carrying swords.

538
00:33:53,510 --> 00:33:55,324
Ninja are even more interesting.

539
00:33:55,364 --> 00:33:58,684
The real shinobi of Iga and
Koga were scouts and saboteurs

540
00:33:58,724 --> 00:34:01,059
of the fifteenth and sixteenth centuries.

541
00:34:01,099 --> 00:34:04,824
And the famous black outfit came
from theater: stagehands dressed

542
00:34:04,864 --> 00:34:07,605
in black, whom the audience
pretends not to see.

543
00:34:09,040 --> 00:34:13,823
Ninja are studied seriously in Japan: Mie
University has a research center and a

544
00:34:13,863 --> 00:34:17,863
master's program, and the first
graduate finished in twenty twenty.

545
00:34:17,903 --> 00:34:20,576
In twenty twenty-two,
an old ninja manual was

546
00:34:20,616 --> 00:34:23,109
unveiled, with a spell to keep dogs quiet.

547
00:34:23,149 --> 00:34:26,184
A ninja's main enemy, it
seems, wasn't the samurai.

548
00:34:26,224 --> 00:34:27,751
It was the neighbor's dog.

549
00:34:28,890 --> 00:34:31,548
But the warrior spirit
lives in gyms, not books.

550
00:34:31,588 --> 00:34:34,864
Over two million people have
earned a dan in kendo, fencing

551
00:34:34,904 --> 00:34:37,617
with bamboo swords, almost
a third of them women.

552
00:34:37,657 --> 00:34:42,338
And since twenty twelve, martial arts have
been required in middle-school gym class.

553
00:34:45,120 --> 00:34:46,085
And there's sumo.

554
00:34:46,125 --> 00:34:50,812
Wrestlers live in shared 'stables', wear
a topknot, and are banned from driving.

555
00:34:50,852 --> 00:34:54,003
Japan's strongest men aren't
allowed behind the wheel.

556
00:34:54,043 --> 00:34:58,849
Fewer and fewer want in: in spring twenty
twenty-six, only twenty recruits joined.

557
00:34:58,889 --> 00:34:59,617
A record low.

558
00:35:02,570 --> 00:35:06,445
Sumo has strict rules. By tradition,
women can't step onto the ring.

559
00:35:06,485 --> 00:35:10,590
In twenty eighteen in Maizuru, the
mayor collapsed there mid-speech, and

560
00:35:10,630 --> 00:35:14,447
female medics treating him were
told over the loudspeaker to leave.

561
00:35:14,487 --> 00:35:17,037
The sumo association's
head later apologized.

562
00:35:18,200 --> 00:35:21,024
The warrior nation's hushed
chapter: World War Two.

563
00:35:22,810 --> 00:35:26,144
About four thousand Japanese
suicide pilots, the kamikaze,

564
00:35:26,184 --> 00:35:28,703
died steering their
planes into enemy ships.

565
00:35:28,743 --> 00:35:32,833
A museum in Kagoshima, in southern
Japan, keeps their farewell letters.

566
00:35:34,270 --> 00:35:36,920
August sixth, nineteen
forty-five: Hiroshima.

567
00:35:36,960 --> 00:35:38,534
Three days later: Nagasaki.

568
00:35:38,574 --> 00:35:42,000
In Hiroshima, by the end of
that year, about a hundred and

569
00:35:42,040 --> 00:35:45,108
forty thousand people had
died from the atomic bomb.

570
00:35:45,148 --> 00:35:46,961
Survivors are called hibakusha.

571
00:35:47,001 --> 00:35:51,922
Today there are over ninety-one thousand,
and their average age is over eighty-six.

572
00:35:51,962 --> 00:35:56,584
In twenty twenty-four, their organization,
Nihon Hidankyo, won the Nobel Peace

573
00:35:56,624 --> 00:36:00,649
Prize, for testifying that nuclear
weapons must never be used again.

574
00:36:02,550 --> 00:36:04,277
Honesty means the whole story.

575
00:36:04,317 --> 00:36:07,810
In that war, Japan caused immense
suffering to other peoples

576
00:36:07,850 --> 00:36:10,696
too: Nanjing, Unit
seven-three-one, forced labor.

577
00:36:10,736 --> 00:36:13,758
How to remember it is still
debated in Japan itself.

578
00:36:16,060 --> 00:36:19,296
For one soldier, the war ended
only in nineteen seventy-four.

579
00:36:19,336 --> 00:36:23,110
Lieutenant Hiroo Onoda fought on for
twenty-nine years in the jungle of

580
00:36:23,150 --> 00:36:26,601
Lubang, in the Philippines, refusing
to believe the war was over.

581
00:36:26,641 --> 00:36:29,286
He surrendered only when
his former commander came

582
00:36:29,326 --> 00:36:31,112
and canceled his orders in person.

583
00:36:31,152 --> 00:36:33,637
But there's another side:
over those years, his

584
00:36:33,677 --> 00:36:35,678
unit killed about thirty local people.

585
00:36:38,820 --> 00:36:40,834
This war also has a northern chapter.

586
00:36:40,874 --> 00:36:44,109
After the war, about five
hundred and seventy-five thousand

587
00:36:44,149 --> 00:36:46,773
Japanese prisoners ended
up in the Soviet Union.

588
00:36:46,813 --> 00:36:49,105
About fifty-five thousand never came home.

589
00:36:49,145 --> 00:36:51,825
Japan still searches for
their remains and brings

590
00:36:51,865 --> 00:36:53,990
them back: over twenty thousand so far.

591
00:36:54,030 --> 00:36:57,431
And there's still no peace treaty
between Japan and Russia: in

592
00:36:57,471 --> 00:37:00,984
nineteen fifty-six they signed
only a declaration, and in twenty

593
00:37:01,024 --> 00:37:03,315
twenty-two Russia walked out of the talks.

594
00:37:05,290 --> 00:37:08,970
And the biggest twist. After the
war, the samurai nation wrote the

595
00:37:09,010 --> 00:37:11,337
renunciation of war into its constitution.

596
00:37:11,377 --> 00:37:14,719
Article Nine hasn't changed
once since nineteen forty-seven.

597
00:37:14,759 --> 00:37:18,721
Over ninety percent of Japanese view
the Self-Defense Forces favorably.

598
00:37:18,761 --> 00:37:23,456
But they don't enlist: in twenty twenty-three,
only half the needed recruits joined.

599
00:37:23,496 --> 00:37:26,217
And in an international
survey, just nine percent

600
00:37:26,257 --> 00:37:28,247
said they'd fight for their country.

601
00:37:30,500 --> 00:37:33,088
Still, defense spending
has already reached two

602
00:37:33,128 --> 00:37:35,548
percent of GDP, two
years ahead of schedule.

603
00:37:35,588 --> 00:37:38,568
After the twenty twenty-six
election, the ruling party

604
00:37:38,608 --> 00:37:40,861
holds over two thirds of the lower house.

605
00:37:40,901 --> 00:37:43,712
But an amendment also needs
two thirds of the upper

606
00:37:43,752 --> 00:37:45,614
house, then a national referendum.

607
00:37:45,654 --> 00:37:48,018
Will Article Nine
change? An open question.

608
00:37:50,050 --> 00:37:52,213
The samurai spirit moved into the office.

609
00:37:52,253 --> 00:37:55,651
Since nineteen seventy-nine, a
thirteen-day management school at

610
00:37:55,691 --> 00:37:58,229
the foot of Mount Fuji
has run like a boot camp.

611
00:37:58,269 --> 00:37:59,626
It's known as 'hell camp'.

612
00:37:59,666 --> 00:38:03,655
And more and more young people can't
take it. They quit. Through an agency.

613
00:38:06,220 --> 00:38:09,197
The object from the opening
keeps showing up. Spotted it?

614
00:38:09,237 --> 00:38:11,049
Drop the timestamp in the comments.

615
00:38:12,090 --> 00:38:14,942
Swords came off the belt
in eighteen seventy-six.

616
00:38:14,982 --> 00:38:16,830
Armor has long stood in museums.

617
00:38:16,870 --> 00:38:21,316
But to take off absolutely everything,
many Tokyoites still go to one place.

618
00:38:21,356 --> 00:38:23,853
And the price there is
set by the governor.

619
00:38:34,210 --> 00:38:37,703
Evening. Above the Tokyo
rooftops, a tall boiler chimney.

620
00:38:37,743 --> 00:38:40,306
That's how you find a bathhouse from afar.

621
00:38:40,346 --> 00:38:44,645
Over the entrance hangs a curtain
with one character: 'yu', hot water.

622
00:38:45,320 --> 00:38:48,327
Shoes go in a wooden locker,
with a notched wooden key.

623
00:38:48,367 --> 00:38:51,707
In old bathhouses, the owner
sits on a high perch overlooking

624
00:38:51,747 --> 00:38:54,256
both the men's and the
women's changing rooms.

625
00:38:54,296 --> 00:38:56,971
Adult entry: six hundred
yen, about four dollars.

626
00:38:57,011 --> 00:39:00,849
The Tokyo governor sets that price,
for every public bath in the city.

627
00:39:05,690 --> 00:39:08,906
In the changing room, everything
comes off. Everything.

628
00:39:08,946 --> 00:39:10,563
You take only a small towel.

629
00:39:10,603 --> 00:39:12,102
Swimsuits are banned here.

630
00:39:13,000 --> 00:39:17,221
First, you wash. Sitting on a low
stool, facing a mirror and a faucet.

631
00:39:17,261 --> 00:39:19,534
Slowly, with soap, to the last bubble.

632
00:39:19,574 --> 00:39:23,734
Buckets clack on tile, water splashes,
echoes under the high ceiling.

633
00:39:23,774 --> 00:39:26,229
Only clean bodies enter the shared water.

634
00:39:27,720 --> 00:39:32,033
Then rinse with hot water from a
bucket, so your body gets used to it.

635
00:39:32,073 --> 00:39:35,017
The towel goes on your
head, never in the water.

636
00:39:35,057 --> 00:39:37,505
Then slowly, very slowly, into the bath.

637
00:39:37,545 --> 00:39:41,547
Old Tokyo baths run hot: often
forty-two degrees Celsius or more.

638
00:39:42,590 --> 00:39:44,786
Silence. Nobody swims or splashes.

639
00:39:44,826 --> 00:39:46,957
Phones stay in the changing room.

640
00:39:46,997 --> 00:39:51,166
And above the water, across the
whole wall: Mount Fuji. A mural.

641
00:39:51,206 --> 00:39:53,074
It's the most common subject.

642
00:39:54,470 --> 00:39:57,959
A retiree, a student and a
construction worker, side by side.

643
00:39:57,999 --> 00:40:01,198
Without clothes, there are
no titles, no business cards.

644
00:40:01,238 --> 00:40:05,941
Behind a five-foot wall is the women's
side, and sometimes you hear them chatting.

645
00:40:07,130 --> 00:40:09,264
But not everyone gets into this water.

646
00:40:09,304 --> 00:40:12,181
In a twenty fifteen survey,
over half of responding

647
00:40:12,221 --> 00:40:14,813
hotels with baths turned
away tattooed guests.

648
00:40:14,853 --> 00:40:19,275
Tattoos long meant yakuza here, though
yakuza numbers are now at a record low.

649
00:40:19,315 --> 00:40:22,707
Got a tattoo? Check the rules
ahead, or book a private bath.

650
00:40:27,790 --> 00:40:31,826
In the Nagano mountains, though,
monkeys are welcome in the hot water.

651
00:40:31,866 --> 00:40:35,844
In Jigokudani park, since nineteen
sixty-four, Japanese macaques have

652
00:40:35,884 --> 00:40:39,164
sat in the hot springs, snow
on their heads, eyes closed.

653
00:40:40,830 --> 00:40:42,945
But people are leaving the bathhouses.

654
00:40:42,985 --> 00:40:46,518
Thirty years ago, Tokyo had about
fifteen hundred public baths.

655
00:40:46,558 --> 00:40:47,878
Now, about four hundred.

656
00:40:47,918 --> 00:40:52,585
The city has even called on visitors to
help: since fall twenty twenty-five, dozens

657
00:40:52,625 --> 00:40:56,555
of baths welcome foreigners, with
multilingual menus and card payment.

658
00:40:57,170 --> 00:41:01,108
After the bath: a small bottle
of cold milk, downed standing up.

659
00:41:01,148 --> 00:41:03,780
Many swear a bath
doesn't count without it.

660
00:41:03,820 --> 00:41:07,820
Then out into the night, steamed
through. The city feels quieter.

661
00:41:08,300 --> 00:41:10,935
When a bathhouse closes for
good, its sign goes dark.

662
00:41:10,975 --> 00:41:13,358
It feels like it's not a
bath that closed, but a

663
00:41:13,398 --> 00:41:15,377
whole neighborhood that stopped meeting.

664
00:41:19,240 --> 00:41:23,405
And sometimes it isn't neighborhoods
that stop meeting. It's people. For good.

665
00:41:31,590 --> 00:41:33,440
Remember the number from the start?

666
00:41:33,480 --> 00:41:36,464
In twenty twenty-five, Japan
logged eighty thousand four

667
00:41:36,504 --> 00:41:38,949
hundred and eighty-six
missing-person reports.

668
00:41:40,600 --> 00:41:42,659
Some of the missing left on their own.

669
00:41:42,699 --> 00:41:45,366
The Japanese call them
johatsu, 'the evaporated'.

670
00:41:45,406 --> 00:41:48,404
They run from debt, from
shame, from domestic violence.

671
00:41:48,444 --> 00:41:51,055
'Night moving' firms help
them, secretly hauling

672
00:41:51,095 --> 00:41:53,264
their belongings away in a single night.

673
00:41:53,304 --> 00:41:56,523
In twenty seventeen, Time
magazine reported a cost of fifty

674
00:41:56,563 --> 00:42:00,114
thousand to three hundred thousand
yen, roughly three hundred and

675
00:42:00,154 --> 00:42:02,931
twenty to nineteen hundred
dollars at today's rate.

676
00:42:04,760 --> 00:42:06,545
Why doesn't anyone look for them?

677
00:42:06,585 --> 00:42:10,472
If an adult left voluntarily and
there's no sign of a crime, the police

678
00:42:10,512 --> 00:42:13,513
barely step in, and privacy
here is strictly protected.

679
00:42:13,553 --> 00:42:17,274
Nobody counts how many of the eighty
thousand actually 'evaporated'.

680
00:42:17,314 --> 00:42:19,597
And 'a hundred thousand vanish forever', a

681
00:42:19,637 --> 00:42:21,920
favorite line of online videos, is a myth.

682
00:42:21,960 --> 00:42:25,348
What's really behind those eighty
thousand? We'll get to that.

683
00:42:27,840 --> 00:42:30,327
There's another kind of
vanishing, a quiet one.

684
00:42:30,367 --> 00:42:33,231
According to the police, in
twenty twenty-five, almost

685
00:42:33,271 --> 00:42:36,404
seventy-seven thousand people
who lived alone died at home.

686
00:42:36,444 --> 00:42:38,932
Over three quarters were
older than sixty-five.

687
00:42:38,972 --> 00:42:42,481
Over twenty-two thousand were found
only after eight days or more.

688
00:42:45,640 --> 00:42:47,458
Miyu Kojima cleans such apartments.

689
00:42:47,498 --> 00:42:49,847
For almost ten years,
she has also built tiny

690
00:42:49,887 --> 00:42:52,077
replicas of them, down to the last detail.

691
00:42:52,117 --> 00:42:55,953
Her miniatures show the problem without
shock, but you won't forget them.

692
00:42:57,450 --> 00:43:00,043
About one and a half million
working-age Japanese have

693
00:43:00,083 --> 00:43:02,091
barely left home for six months or longer.

694
00:43:02,131 --> 00:43:03,358
They're called hikikomori.

695
00:43:03,398 --> 00:43:07,113
And when parents in their eighties
support a son or daughter in their fifties

696
00:43:07,153 --> 00:43:10,379
who won't leave their room, it's
called the 'eighty-fifty problem'.

697
00:43:14,820 --> 00:43:17,189
And here's a story that's hard to forget.

698
00:43:17,229 --> 00:43:21,771
In Japan's largest women's prison, in
Tochigi, one in five inmates is elderly.

699
00:43:21,811 --> 00:43:23,710
One of them is Akiyo, eighty-one.

700
00:43:23,750 --> 00:43:25,414
She's here for stealing food.

701
00:43:25,454 --> 00:43:29,233
Over eighty percent of elderly
female prisoners are in for theft.

702
00:43:29,273 --> 00:43:32,934
Some admit to journalists that
prison feels 'more stable': here

703
00:43:32,974 --> 00:43:35,637
they get food, doctors
and someone to talk to.

704
00:43:35,677 --> 00:43:39,632
And a prison officer said some are
even willing to pay just to stay.

705
00:43:43,950 --> 00:43:45,472
Houses are vanishing too.

706
00:43:45,512 --> 00:43:48,785
A twenty twenty-three survey
found nine million empty

707
00:43:48,825 --> 00:43:51,034
homes in Japan, almost one in seven.

708
00:43:51,074 --> 00:43:54,909
Local 'empty house banks' even
list homes for zero yen: owners

709
00:43:54,949 --> 00:43:57,721
are glad to dodge taxes
and demolition bills.

710
00:43:58,660 --> 00:44:01,646
In the village of Nagoro,
dolls replaced the people.

711
00:44:01,686 --> 00:44:04,265
Ayano Tsukimi returned
from Osaka and sewed a

712
00:44:04,305 --> 00:44:06,709
life-size scarecrow resembling her father.

713
00:44:06,749 --> 00:44:09,386
Then her neighbors who
had moved away or died.

714
00:44:09,426 --> 00:44:12,644
Now a few dozen people live
here, and hundreds of dolls.

715
00:44:12,684 --> 00:44:15,903
In the shuttered school, doll
students sit at the desks.

716
00:44:16,780 --> 00:44:18,743
That's how the whole country is aging.

717
00:44:18,783 --> 00:44:21,747
In twenty twenty-five, Japanese
citizens had fewer babies

718
00:44:21,787 --> 00:44:24,066
than in any year since
eighteen ninety-nine.

719
00:44:24,106 --> 00:44:27,123
Centenarians topped a hundred
thousand for the first time.

720
00:44:27,163 --> 00:44:30,181
And the top maker sells more
adult diapers than baby ones.

721
00:44:32,140 --> 00:44:34,294
A hundred thousand centenarians: how?

722
00:44:34,334 --> 00:44:38,444
Japanese women live past eighty-seven
on average, men past eighty-one.

723
00:44:38,484 --> 00:44:43,129
Okinawa was even named a 'Blue Zone', a
place where people live unusually long.

724
00:44:43,169 --> 00:44:46,271
But since Okinawans took to
American food, the island

725
00:44:46,311 --> 00:44:48,761
has slid noticeably down Japan's rankings.

726
00:44:48,801 --> 00:44:51,489
The Japanese themselves
often mention the word

727
00:44:51,529 --> 00:44:54,216
'ikigai', 'a reason to
get up in the morning'.

728
00:44:54,256 --> 00:44:58,723
Scientists from Tohoku followed forty-three
thousand adults for seven years:

729
00:44:58,763 --> 00:45:02,636
those who said they had no ikigai
had noticeably higher mortality.

730
00:45:02,676 --> 00:45:06,194
And the trendy four-circle
diagram was invented in the West.

731
00:45:06,234 --> 00:45:09,218
In Japan, ikigai is a modest
word: grandchildren, a

732
00:45:09,258 --> 00:45:12,360
vegetable patch, morning
radio exercises in the park.

733
00:45:12,400 --> 00:45:15,444
But these elders have fewer
and fewer grandchildren.

734
00:45:20,300 --> 00:45:24,857
Even the pandas left. Since nineteen
seventy-two, when Japan and China restored

735
00:45:24,897 --> 00:45:27,476
relations, pandas were
the nation's darlings.

736
00:45:27,516 --> 00:45:32,073
In June twenty twenty-five, four pandas
from a park in Shirahama flew to China.

737
00:45:32,113 --> 00:45:36,495
The last ones left were the twins Xiao
Xiao and Lei Lei at Tokyo's Ueno Zoo.

738
00:45:36,535 --> 00:45:38,299
People lined up to say goodbye.

739
00:45:38,339 --> 00:45:41,791
On January twenty-seventh,
twenty twenty-six, they left too.

740
00:45:41,831 --> 00:45:45,573
For the first time in half a
century, Japan has no pandas at all.

741
00:45:48,050 --> 00:45:50,858
Pandas belong to China
and are only on loan.

742
00:45:50,898 --> 00:45:54,158
No new pair yet: analysts
link it to Prime Minister

743
00:45:54,198 --> 00:45:56,423
Sanae Takaichi's remarks on Taiwan.

744
00:45:58,410 --> 00:46:01,665
People leave, houses empty
out, even pandas fly away.

745
00:46:01,705 --> 00:46:03,841
They say this country is vanishing.

746
00:46:03,881 --> 00:46:07,634
But for a few summer days, even
those who are gone come home.

747
00:46:18,720 --> 00:46:24,370
Darkness. A drumbeat. And out of the dark
floats a paper warrior as tall as a house.

748
00:46:24,410 --> 00:46:27,554
Light burns inside it:
lamps behind thin paper.

749
00:46:27,594 --> 00:46:29,993
Aomori, August, the Nebuta festival.

750
00:46:32,520 --> 00:46:35,092
You rent a dancer's
costume, for about four

751
00:46:35,132 --> 00:46:37,583
thousand yen, around twenty-five dollars.

752
00:46:37,623 --> 00:46:41,593
A white yukata, a colorful hat,
and nobody asks where you're from.

753
00:46:41,633 --> 00:46:44,266
The drums pound so hard
your chest vibrates.

754
00:46:44,306 --> 00:46:46,939
You jump with the crowd
and shout: 'Rassera!

755
00:46:46,979 --> 00:46:50,767
Rassera!' A minute ago you were
a spectator. Now you're inside.

756
00:46:54,060 --> 00:46:58,396
Giant figures float past: samurai,
demons, gods with faces meters tall.

757
00:46:58,436 --> 00:47:01,724
On the last night, the best
of them sail out to sea on

758
00:47:01,764 --> 00:47:04,312
boats, and fireworks burst over the water.

759
00:47:05,310 --> 00:47:08,691
Craftsmen build every such figure
from wire, paper and light.

760
00:47:08,731 --> 00:47:11,328
But festivals are aging
along with the country.

761
00:47:11,368 --> 00:47:16,207
In twenty twenty-four, the 'Naked Festival'
in Konomiya let women in for the first time

762
00:47:16,247 --> 00:47:20,358
in twelve hundred and fifty years: in
festival coats, away from the crush.

763
00:47:20,398 --> 00:47:22,938
The reason is simple:
there aren't enough men.

764
00:47:27,030 --> 00:47:28,671
And in mid-August comes Obon.

765
00:47:28,711 --> 00:47:32,555
By tradition, the souls of ancestors
return home during these days.

766
00:47:32,595 --> 00:47:35,280
People light fires for
them and dance for them.

767
00:47:35,320 --> 00:47:39,569
And on the last evening, they see them
off, setting paper lanterns afloat.

768
00:47:39,609 --> 00:47:42,410
In Hiroshima, lanterns
float down the river every

769
00:47:42,450 --> 00:47:44,497
August sixth, in memory of the dead.

770
00:47:46,780 --> 00:47:48,412
And now the truth we promised.

771
00:47:48,452 --> 00:47:51,310
Remember the eighty thousand
missing-person reports?

772
00:47:51,350 --> 00:47:54,263
Most of those people are
found, and most often alive.

773
00:47:54,303 --> 00:47:56,659
Over thirty-three
thousand on the same day.

774
00:47:56,699 --> 00:47:59,390
Another twenty thousand
within two or three days.

775
00:47:59,430 --> 00:48:03,960
And last year, fourteen hundred and four
people turned up after two years or more.

776
00:48:08,760 --> 00:48:12,613
One more quiet piece of news:
preliminary data show births in Japan

777
00:48:12,653 --> 00:48:16,506
rose slightly in the first half of
twenty twenty-six, for the first

778
00:48:16,546 --> 00:48:19,295
time in eleven years,
even excluding foreigners.

779
00:48:19,335 --> 00:48:22,200
The country every video
buries isn't finished yet.

780
00:48:23,740 --> 00:48:27,195
But for those who can't be
found, people still come back.

781
00:48:27,235 --> 00:48:31,978
On a hill in the town of Otsuchi, above
the ocean, stands a white phone booth.

782
00:48:32,018 --> 00:48:35,167
Inside is an old rotary
phone, connected to nothing.

783
00:48:35,207 --> 00:48:37,988
Gardener Itaru Sasaki
set it up in twenty ten,

784
00:48:38,028 --> 00:48:40,318
after his cousin died, to talk to him.

785
00:48:40,358 --> 00:48:42,526
Three months later came the tsunami.

786
00:48:43,730 --> 00:48:47,252
The twenty eleven tsunami took
almost sixteen thousand lives.

787
00:48:47,292 --> 00:48:50,172
Over twenty-five hundred
people are still missing.

788
00:48:50,212 --> 00:48:53,676
And people began coming to the
booth on the hill. Thousands.

789
00:48:53,716 --> 00:48:57,414
They step in, lift the receiver,
and talk to those the sea took.

790
00:48:59,200 --> 00:49:01,909
A few years later, a student
from Sendai interviewed

791
00:49:01,949 --> 00:49:04,130
over a hundred taxi drivers in Ishinomaki.

792
00:49:04,170 --> 00:49:08,254
Seven told of passengers who got into
the car and vanished from the back seat.

793
00:49:08,294 --> 00:49:10,369
And some drivers ran the meter for them.

794
00:49:15,840 --> 00:49:18,208
Now, the thing almost
nobody comes back for.

795
00:49:18,248 --> 00:49:21,054
You've seen it in every
chapter. The clear umbrella.

796
00:49:21,094 --> 00:49:23,954
Of every hundred handed in
to Tokyo's lost and found,

797
00:49:23,994 --> 00:49:26,033
about one gets picked up by its owner.

798
00:49:26,073 --> 00:49:28,113
The rest never see their owners again.

799
00:49:28,153 --> 00:49:30,411
You can buy a new one at any corner store.

800
00:49:32,500 --> 00:49:34,738
Everything else, people come back for.

801
00:49:34,778 --> 00:49:38,695
Not always: we saw the empty houses,
and those who chose to leave.

802
00:49:38,735 --> 00:49:42,053
But wallets get claimed.
Ancestors get their Obon fires.

803
00:49:42,093 --> 00:49:44,691
People travel to the
hill to talk once more.

804
00:49:44,731 --> 00:49:46,909
And some are found, even years later.

805
00:49:49,270 --> 00:49:51,109
Maybe that's the Japanese secret.

806
00:49:51,149 --> 00:49:52,988
Let go of what you can buy again.

807
00:49:53,028 --> 00:49:56,347
Come back for what you can't:
a person, a voice, a meeting.

808
00:49:56,387 --> 00:50:00,447
Though here it can go the other way
too: a stranger's wallet comes back,

809
00:50:00,487 --> 00:50:03,863
yet the neighbor behind the
wall may go unnoticed for weeks.

810
00:50:03,903 --> 00:50:06,596
A meeting happens once,
but only if you show up.

811
00:50:06,636 --> 00:50:10,638
The host bows at the tea-room door,
and that meeting never comes again.

812
00:50:10,678 --> 00:50:13,087
Ichigo ichie. What
would you come back for?

813
00:50:20,010 --> 00:50:22,681
Next time, we fly somewhere
completely different.

814
00:50:22,721 --> 00:50:26,886
Where everything is said out loud, and
nobody apologizes for twenty seconds.
